:name: ArduPilot i.MX RT1176
:description: boots an AP_HAL_Zephyr i.MX RT1176 firmware ELF

# The CM7 in an RT1176 is the same Cortex-M7 core as an STM32H7, so the CPU
# needs nothing special. Only the memory map and the peripherals differ, and
# Renode already ships every NXP IP model the platform uses.
#
# Unlike the STM32 scripts there is no generated per-board overlay: gen_board.py
# reads ChibiOS hwdef, and this board is Zephyr-only. $platform is the static
# base platform, and $elf is the built Zephyr image.

$repo?=@.
$elf?=@none
$platform?=$repo/Tools/renode/platforms/imxrt1176_base.repl

# Board-agnostic helpers, shared with the STM32 scripts.
include $repo/Tools/renode/peripherals/common/AP_NVIC_RettobaseFix.cs
include $repo/Tools/renode/peripherals/common/AP_DWT.cs
include $repo/Tools/renode/peripherals/nxp/AP_IMXRT_PowerSequencing.cs
include $repo/Tools/renode/peripherals/nxp/AP_IMXRT_CCM.cs
include $repo/Tools/renode/peripherals/nxp/AP_IMXRT_USBPHY.cs
include $repo/Tools/renode/peripherals/nxp/AP_IMXRT_FlexCAN.cs
include $repo/Tools/renode/peripherals/nxp/AP_IMXRT_USDHC.cs
# The classic eDMA + DMAMUX, and the LPUART helper that gives Renode's
# NXP_LPUART a usable RX DMA request and an idle line. These two do not
# reference each other, so their order here does not matter - the order that
# does is inside AP_IMXRT_EDMA.cs, which holds both the eDMA and the DMAMUX in
# one file because the ad-hoc compiler builds each .cs against only the ones
# included before it.
include $repo/Tools/renode/peripherals/nxp/AP_IMXRT_EDMA.cs
include $repo/Tools/renode/peripherals/nxp/AP_IMXRT_LPUART_DmaFix.cs

# The lockstep physics bridge and the sensor models it drives, in dependency
# order: the ad-hoc compiler builds each .cs against only the ones included
# before it, so AP_Physics.cs (which defines AP_PhysicsState, AP_PhysicsActuator
# and the sensor-orientation helper) has to come before anything that reads
# physics truth, and the two device base classes before the devices.
include $repo/Tools/renode/peripherals/common/AP_Physics.cs
include $repo/Tools/renode/peripherals/common/AP_I2CRegisterDevice.cs
include $repo/Tools/renode/peripherals/common/AP_UARTFrameDevice.cs
# AP_SPIMultiplexer implements IAPSigrok, which is declared in the interface
# file below; the generated STM32 platforms get it from their wrapper script,
# and a static platform has to ask for it itself.
include $repo/Tools/renode/peripherals/common/AP_SigrokInterface.cs
include $repo/Tools/renode/peripherals/common/AP_SPIMultiplexer.cs

# The three i.MX RT buses ArduPilot's sensors are on. LPSPI and LPI2C are ours
# because Renode's LPSPI has no DMA request output and Renode has no LPI2C at
# all; FlexPWM is ours because it is also the actuator tap.
include $repo/Tools/renode/peripherals/nxp/AP_IMXRT_LPSPI.cs
include $repo/Tools/renode/peripherals/nxp/AP_IMXRT_LPI2C.cs
include $repo/Tools/renode/peripherals/nxp/AP_IMXRT_FlexPWM.cs

# The sensors themselves, shared with the STM32 boards.
include $repo/Tools/renode/peripherals/sensors/AP_ICM42688.cs
include $repo/Tools/renode/peripherals/sensors/AP_BMP388.cs
include $repo/Tools/renode/peripherals/sensors/AP_BMM150.cs
include $repo/Tools/renode/peripherals/sensors/AP_UBlox.cs

mach create "imxrt1176"
machine LoadPlatformDescription $platform

# The application executes in place from the FlexSPI window rather than from
# internal flash, so the ELF's own load addresses are already correct.
sysbus LoadELF $elf

# The image XIPs from the FlexSPI window above the bootloader, so the reset
# vector is not at address 0. Point the core at the application vector table,
# and again after every reset - ArduPilot reboots itself via NVIC_SystemReset
# and Renode otherwise restores the default table at 0.
$vector_base?=0x30022000
cpu VectorTableOffset $vector_base

macro reset
"""
    cpu VectorTableOffset $vector_base
"""

# GPS on SERIAL3 = lpuart3. A hub rather than a direct connection because that
# is what Renode's UART connector takes, and it is the shape the generated STM32
# platforms use for the same model. Created here so every user of this script
# gets the GPS: a boot check sees a board with a GPS on it, and the flight test
# needs no extra wiring of its own.
emulation CreateUARTHub "gpsHub"
connector Connect sysbus.lpuart3 gpsHub
connector Connect sysbus.gps gpsHub

# No showAnalyzer here: this script is run headless from CI. The caller
# attaches the console UART to whatever it wants - a socket terminal for
# zephyr_boot_check.py, a window when run by hand.

# 1GHz Cortex-M7, derated for XIP fetch from external NOR and bus contention.
#
# Scaled to match the CubeOrange platform, so the two boards' flights are
# comparable rather than accidentally different: that one gives a guest which
# believes it runs at 480 MHz (CONFIG_SYS_CLOCK_HW_CYCLES_PER_SEC) a budget of
# 300 MIPS, i.e. 0.625 instructions per believed CPU cycle. This guest believes
# 1 GHz, so the same ratio is 625.
#
# The old value of 400 was 0.40 instructions per believed cycle - a third less
# CPU per guest-second than the CubeOrange gets - and it showed: with the IMU
# finally streaming, the main loop reached 39 Hz against a commanded
# SCHED_LOOP_RATE of 125 while the CubeOrange holds 125 on the same mission.
cpu PerformanceInMips 625

emulation SetGlobalQuantum "0.01"

# Pace virtual time against wall clock, as the STM32 scripts do. Without this
# the board's own clock outruns real time - 4386 s of board time in 592 s of
# wall time, measured 2026-09-13 - because virtual time is minted faster than
# the work behind it, and every rate the firmware reports in board-seconds
# (SCHED_LOOP_RATE above all) is then divided by that factor. With it, one
# board second is one real second and the loop rate means what it says.
#
# It only sleeps when virtual time is AHEAD; emulation that cannot keep up is
# left unrestricted, so this cannot make the board slower than the host allows.
#
# (An early version of this script paired `false` with a machine that had not
# been started, and the time framework sat in WaitingForReportBack with virtual
# time at zero - that was the missing `start`, not this setting.)
emulation SetGlobalAdvanceImmediately false
